" (https://www.supremecourt.gov/filingandrules/ElectronicFilingGuidelines_March2026.pdf)\nciteturn1236view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 6; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1225view1\",\"id\":10}); Total lines: 187\nL0@P0: SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES\nL1@P0: OFFICE OF THE CLERK\nL2@P0: WASHINGTON, D.C. 20543\nL3@P0: GUIDELINES FOR THE SUBMISSION OF DOCUMENTS TO\nL4@P0: THE SUPREME COURT’S ELECTRONIC FILING SYSTEM\nL5@P0: \nL6@P0: These guidelines govern the submission of documents to the electronic filing\nL7@P0: system at the Supreme Court of the United States. They are issued pursuant to\nL8@P0: Supreme Court Rule 29.7.\nL9@P0: 1. Electronic Submission Requirement. Filings submitted by parties\nL10@P0: represented by counsel (with the exception of material addressed in paragraphs 7, 9,\nL11@P0: 14 and 15 below) must be submitted through the Court’s electronic filing system.\nL12@P0: This requirement is in addition to the existing requirements concerning the paper\nL13@P0: filing of documents with the Court. This requirement applies to all documents\nL14@P0: required or permitted to be presented to the Court or a Justice, unless otherwise\nL15@P0: directed by Court Rule, these Guidelines, or other communication from the Clerk’s\nL16@P0: Office. Documents should be submitted through the electronic filing system\nL17@P0: contemporaneously with their filing pursuant to Rule 29.2, i.e., at or near the time\nL18@P0: they are delivered to the Court, placed in the mail, delivered to a third-party carrier,\nL19@P0: etc. Filers relying upon Rule 29.2(4) to establish the timeliness of a filing are\nL20@P0: reminded that the filing will not be accepted unless the paper version is timely\nL21@P0: submitted through one of the methods in Rules 29.2(1)-(3).\nL22@P0: 2. Registration. Before submitting documents through the electronic\nL23@P0: filing system, attorneys must register at file.supremecourt.gov/Account/Register.\nL24@P0: Only members of the Supreme Court Bar and attorneys appointed for a particular\nL25@P0-1: case under the federal Criminal Justice Act are eligible to register. As part of the - 2 -\nL26@P1: registration process, an attorney will establish a username and password that will\nL27@P1: enable the attorney to submit documents through the system. Users must protect the\nL28@P1: security of their username and password, and must notify the Clerk’s Office\nL29@P1: immediately upon learning that either has been compromised. Users also have a\nL30@P1: responsibility to keep contact information up-to-date; changes to contact information\nL31@P1: can be made through the “My Account” link on the electronic filing system home\nL32@P1: page.\nL33@P1: 3. Notice of Appearance. Attorneys are required to enter a notice of\nL34@P1: appearance in a case before submitting filings, including waivers, on behalf of a\nL35@P1: party or amicus curiae in that case. The submission of a case-initiating document\nL36@P1: (e.g., a petition for a writ of certiorari, a jurisdictional statement, a petition for an\nL37@P1: extraordinary writ, or an application not connected to an existing case) will serve as\nL38@P1: a notice of appearance for the filer. But a filer seeking to submit any other filings in\nL39@P1: a case will be required to enter a notice of appearance in that case. The notice of\nL40@P1: appearance is created and submitted entirely though the system itself; no separate\nL41@P1: paper document need be submitted, and no PDF need be uploaded. While it is\nL42@P1: permissible for multiple attorneys to submit notices of appearance on behalf of the\nL43@P1: same party, a party may have only one counsel of record. If an attorney no longer\nL44@P1: represents a party in this Court, the attorney should submit a withdrawal of\nL45@P1: appearance through the system. This withdrawal will also be entirely electronic; no\nL46@P1: paper document need be submitted, and no PDF need be uploaded.\nL47@P1: 4. Format of Documents. Documents submitted through the electronic\nL48@P1: filing system should be in the PDF/A format. Where possible, the electronic file\nL49@P1: should be created from a word processing document and should be text searchable.\nL50@P1: Where practical, the electronic version of a document with a signature (including\nL51@P1: letters to the Court, certificates of compliance with word limitations under Rule\nL52@P1: 33(h), and proofs of service under Rule 29.5) should be created from a word\nL53@P1: processing document and be text searchable; the version submitted through the\nL54@P1: electronic filing system need not contain a physical signature. Items included in an\nL55@P1: appendix to a filing may be scanned if a word-processing document is not available.\nL56@P1: A document submitted through the electronic filing system may contain hyperlinks\nL57@P1: to another part of the same document, or to an external source cited in the document.\nL58@P1: 5. Maximum Size of Documents. The maximum size of any single\nL59@P1-2: computer file that can be uploaded to the electronic filing system is 100MB. - 3 -\nL60@P2: Documents larger than 100MB should be separated into multiple parts to allow each\nL61@P2: part to be under this limit.\nL62@P2: 6. Viruses and Malware. Before submitting any document through the\nL63@P2: electronic filing system, the filer should ensure that the document has been scanned\nL64@P2: for viruses and malware. The electronic filing system will also perform a scan for\nL65@P2: viruses and malware.\nL66@P2: 7. Sealed Material. Filings containing any material that is under seal,\nL67@P2: including documents filed under seal in lower courts and motions to file documents\nL68@P2: under seal in this Court, should not be submitted through the electronic filing system.\nL69@P2: Those documents (including related public documents such as redacted versions for\nL70@P2: the public record) should be submitted only in paper form. Thus if a public petition\nL71@P2: includes a public appendix and a supplemental appendix to be filed under seal, all of\nL72@P2: these documents should be filed in paper form only. Such documents also should\nL73@P2: not be served upon other parties electronically. Filers are admonished to pay strict\nL74@P2: attention to the prohibition, since documents submitted through the electronic filing\nL75@P2: system may be posted publicly before personal review by Clerk’s Office staff.\nL76@P2: 8. Redaction of Personal Identifying Information. Personal identifying\nL77@P2: information contained in filings must be redacted in keeping with the standards set\nL78@P2: forth in Rule 34.6. No motion is required to make redactions to conform with this\nL79@P2: rule. Nothing in the rule precludes a party from filing a motion to redact additional\nL80@P2: information in appropriate circumstances. The responsibility to redact this\nL81@P2: information rests with counsel and the parties.\nL82@P2: 9. Cases Governed by Fed. R. Civ. P. 5.2(c). In cases governed below\nL83@P2: by Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 5.2(c), including immigration cases addressed in\nL84@P2: Federal Rule of Criminal Procedure 49.1(c), filings by the parties should not be\nL85@P2: submitted through the electronic filing system. A cover letter provided along with\nL86@P2: the petition for a writ of certiorari, application for an extension of time to file a\nL87@P2: petition for a writ of certiorari, or other case-initiating document should clearly\nL88@P2: state that the case was governed by Rule 5.2(c).\nL89@P2: 10. Posting of Documents. Electronic versions of all documents filed with\nL90@P2: the Court (except those containing sealed material or otherwise exempt from\nL91@P2: electronic posting) will be made available to the public without charge on the\nL92@P2-3: Supreme Court’s website at http://www.supremecourt.gov. - 4 -\nL93@P3: (a) Filings that initiate a new case at the Supreme Court will be\nL94@P3: posted on the Court’s website only after the Clerk’s Office has received\nL95@P3: and reviewed the paper version of the filing, determined that it should be\nL96@P3: accepted for filing, and assigned a case number.\nL97@P3: (b) Subsequent filings from represented parties that are submitted\nL98@P3: through the electronic filing system will be posted upon electronic\nL99@P3: submission to the system. Such filings will initially be noted on the docket\nL100@P3: as “Submitted.” Once the Clerk’s Office has received and reviewed the\nL101@P3: paper version of the document and determined that it should be accepted\nL102@P3: for filing, the docket will reflect that the document has been “Filed.” If a\nL103@P3: document is not accepted for filing, the docket entry will reflect that it is\nL104@P3: “Not Accepted for Filing,” and an electronic version of the document will\nL105@P3: no longer be accessible.\nL106@P3: (c) Paper filings from parties not represented by counsel will be\nL107@P3: scanned by the Clerk’s Office and posted on the Court’s website once the\nL108@P3: Clerk’s Office has reviewed the filing and determined that it should be\nL109@P3: accepted for filing.\nL110@P3: 11. Service and Notification of Case Activity. Registered users who have\nL111@P3: entered an appearance in a given case will receive automated email notification of\nL112@P3: all action in that case, including filings by other parties. This notification does not\nL113@P3: constitute official service, and parties remain obligated to effect formal service as\nL114@P3: outlined in the Rules of the Court. Attorneys can ensure their legal team receives\nL115@P3: notification of case activity by adding their email addresses to the notifications\nL116@P3: screen on their notice of appearance, or by registering for email notifications on the\nL117@P3: docket of a particular case.\nL118@P3: Where a filer relies upon Rule 29.2(4) for the timeliness of its filing but the\nL119@P3: paper version is submitted to the Court within three days, the certificate of service\nL120@P3: should reflect both electronic transmission of the document to other parties and the\nL121@P3: expected service of the paper version. For example, the certificate could read as\nL122@P3: follows:\nL123@P3: I certify that on [date], I served an electronic version of the petition for\nL124@P3: a writ of certiorari in the above-captioned matter upon the following,\nL125@P3-4: and that pursuant to Rule 29.2, three copies of a paper version will be - 5 -\nL126@P4: sent to the same individuals via United States mail, postage prepaid by\nL127@P4: [date]:\nL128@P4: 12. Changes to Filings. Once a document is submitted through the\nL129@P4: electronic filing system, the filer will no longer have the ability to withdraw the\nL130@P4: electronic filing or make changes to it through the system. In the event that a filer\nL131@P4: needs to make a change to the document that was submitted, the filer should contact\nL132@P4: the Clerk’s Office.\nL133@P4: 13. Filing Fees. Filers submitting through the electronic filing system will\nL134@P4: continue to pay filing fees, where applicable, by check. The check should be\nL135@P4: included with the official paper version of the filing, and should be made payable to\nL136@P4: the Supreme Court of the United States. If a fee is required, the filing will not be\nL137@P4: docketed until the fee is received.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.supremecourt.gov/casehand/PaidCasesGuide2026.pdf)\nciteturn1236view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 11; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1225view1\",\"id\":11}); Total lines: 274\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.supremecourt.gov/casehand/IFPGuide2026.pdf)\nciteturn1236view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 25; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1225view1\",\"id\":12}); Total lines: 460\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.supremecourt.gov/casehand/AmicusGuide2026.pdf)\nciteturn1236view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 6; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1225view1\",\"id\":13}); Total lines: 159\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGuide to Filing Paid Cases (https://www.supremecourt.gov/casehand/GuidanceOnScheduling2026.pdf)\nciteturn1236view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 5; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1225view1\",\"id\":14}); Total lines: 133\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGuide for Counsel in Cases to be Argued (October Term 2024) (https://www.supremecourt.gov/casehand/Guide%20for%20Counsel%202024.pdf)\nciteturn1236view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 23; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1225view1\",\"id\":15}); Total lines: 595\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.supremecourt.gov/casehand/BookletFormatSpecificChart2026.pdf)\nciteturn1236view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 1; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1225view1\",\"id\":16}); Total lines: 63\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDelivery of Documents to the Clerk's Office - Supreme Court of the United States (https://www.supremecourt.gov/deliveryofdocuments.aspx)\nciteturn1236view7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1225view1\",\"id\":17}); Total lines: 82\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.supremecourt.gov/casehand/waiver.pdf)\nciteturn1236view8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 1; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1225view1\",\"id\":18}); Total lines: 30\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nArgument Form (https://www.supremecourt.gov/oral_arguments/Argument%20Form.pdf)\nciteturn1236view9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 1; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1225view1\",\"id\":19}); Total lines: 37\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMiscellaneous Order (09/28/2022) (https://www.supremecourt.gov/orders/courtorders/092822zr_1b72.pdf)\nciteturn1236view10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 1; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1225view1\",\"id\":20}); Total lines: 23\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCircuit Map (06/30/2022) (https://www.supremecourt.gov/about/Circuit_Map.pdf)\nciteturn1236view11 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 1; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1225view1\",\"id\":21}); Total lines: 16\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCase Distribution Schedule - Supreme Court of the United States (https://www.supremecourt.gov/casedistribution/casedistributionschedule.aspx)\nciteturn1236view12 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1225view1\",\"id\":22}); Total lines: 93\n"
